Two jobs rather than one, justified briefly: a
scheduler interval is fixed per job, the window has
exactly two base cadences, and a single
10-minute-interval job computing phase in its body
would need ~432 dispatches to yield ~22 checks —
allowance spent on no-ops and a run history too
noisy to cross-check. The tightened cadences are
supervised because the conditions that trigger them
(W-1p, W-3u) are themselves reported to Julie
immediately (§3.4): a tightened phase coincides
with an attended, escalated state by construction,
so its 10-minute checks are performed by Wren in
session and logged in the same window log with the
performer named ("supervised"). The scheduled base
cadence continues underneath throughout. Phase is
computed by each run from the **recorded window-open
timestamp** (written into the window log at Stage
2): nominal points derive from window open + the
table; performed checks are logged with actual
times; coverage and gaps are measured
actual-to-actual (below), so scheduler phase offset
is immaterial to the accounting.

**Handoff (+12 h).** The first hourly-job run at
elapsed ≥ 12 h performs its check in full, then
enables the 6-hourly job, verifies it enabled, then
disables the hourly job, and logs **HANDOFF** with
all three outcomes. If any handoff step fails, the
hourly job remains enabled and hourly coverage
continues; the failure is logged and reported, and
the next hourly run retries. A failed handoff
cannot silently create a gap: coverage continues at
the denser cadence until a handoff succeeds.

