**Per-check logging (the coverage rule).** Every
check, at execution time, appends one entry to the
**window log**: nominal (scheduled) time, actual
time, performer (job id, or "supervised" with the
session named), the full raw §3.1 values, the
classification, and any report made. The window log
is evidence, filed with the window record. **A
check that left no log entry is a MISSED check by
definition** — performance is the log, not the
intention. A check whose entry exists but whose
§3.1 set is incomplete (any read errored or
unavailable) is a **FAILED check**, logged as failed
with the failing surfaces named; a failed check is
**not a negative observation** (it observed nothing
about those surfaces) and counts as a missed check
for gap purposes, with the distinction recorded.

**Two-record cross-check (gap detection).** The
window log is not the only record: the scheduler
keeps its own **run history** per job (scheduled
times, statuses, trigger reasons), independently
queryable at any time. Each scheduled run, before
performing its own check, reads the window-log tail
**and** its job's run history, and reconciles them.
A scheduled point with no performed check therefore
resolves into a named shape:

- **Run record "succeeded", no log entry** —
  **LOG-FAILURE**: the run executed but its logging
  step did not commit; the check's results are
  unrecoverable. Counts as a missed check; the
  discrepancy itself is logged by the detecting run
  and reported.
- **No run record for an elapsed nominal point** —
  **SCHEDULER-GAP**: the job did not run at all.
  Named as such; implicates the scheduler/runtime,
  not the check procedure.
- **Run record "failed"** — a **FAILED run**,
  treated as a failed check (above).

Supervised checks have no run record; their
evidence is the log entry alone, with the performer
named — stated so the asymmetry is on the record,
not discovered later.

**Coverage accounting.** An interval between two
consecutive completed logged checks is a **VERIFIED
interval**: classifications may span it. Any span
containing a missed or failed check is an
**UNOBSERVED interval**, named as such in the
window record with its bounds. Consequences:

- **W-0 ("no event") may be claimed only over
  verified intervals.** A window whose close is
  otherwise W-0 but which contains unobserved
  intervals closes with the W-0 claim **qualified to
  the verified intervals**, the gaps named; the
  NEGATIVE disposition (§4.3) is unavailable on
  gapped coverage unless Rowan dispositions the
  gaps expressly.
- An event whose first evidence appears at the
  check ending an unobserved interval is classified
  on its observed values, with its **arrival time
  recorded as unknown-within the gap**.

